<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1689</h1><p class="ref-lede">1689 in world history: 20 events, 7 births, 2 deaths, 3 works and 2 states that came to an end. 1,147 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1685">1685</a><a href="/years/1686">1686</a><a href="/years/1687">1687</a><a href="/years/1688">1688</a><a href="/years/1689" aria-current="page">1689</a><a href="/years/1690">1690</a><a href="/years/1691">1691</a><a href="/years/1692">1692</a><a href="/years/1693">1693</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-13">13 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">William III and Mary II offered the English crown</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">At the Banqueting House, the Convention Parliament presented the Declaration of Rights and offered the throne jointly to William and Mary. The settlement limited royal power and confirmed Parliament's role.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-11">11 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">William III and Mary II are crowned joint monarchs</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">William and Mary were crowned together in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> after accepting the Declaration of Rights. The settlement confirmed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a> and the limits Parliament had placed on the Crown.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-18">18 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Boston revolts against the Dominion of New England</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Militia and citizens in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, prompted by news of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a>, rose against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-andros">Sir Edmund Andros</a>, governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, and arrested dominion officials. Leaders of the former Massachusetts Bay Colony then reclaimed the government.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-24">24 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Toleration Act grants freedom of worship to Dissenters</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Act allowed Protestant Nonconformists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to worship in their own licensed meeting houses. Catholics and anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinitarians">Trinitarians</a> were excluded.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-27">27 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Jacobites defeat a government army at Killiecrankie</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">In the Jacobite rising of 1689, a Jacobite force led by Ewan Cameron of Lochiel and Viscount Dundee defeated a Scottish government army under Lieutenant-general Hugh Mackay. The rising followed the deposition of King James VII in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-21">21 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Cameronians repel the Jacobites at Dunkeld</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A small regiment of Cameronians held the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkeld">Dunkeld</a> against a much larger Jacobite force. The defeat broke the momentum of the rising that had followed Killiecrankie.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/lachine-massacre">Lachine massacre</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Lachine massacre was a surprise attack by about 1,500 Iroquois warriors on the French settlement of Lachine, on the island of Montreal, on 5 August 1689.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty · Peace</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nerchinsk">Treaty of Nerchinsk</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Treaty of Nerchinsk, signed on 27 August 1689 at Nerchinsk in eastern Siberia, was the first treaty between Russia and Qing China.</p></div></li></ol></section>
